 "$comment": [
  "SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH -- EDIT THIS FILE.",
  "What every row-ID prefix of the documentation MEANS, and which tree owns it. `words` is the English the letters stand for, `means` is one line saying what the numbered rows index, `owner` is the tree that DEFINES them, `mirrored_in` names a tree that only RECORDS them, `pending` marks a prefix registered before its rows exist.",
  "Generated FROM this file: _assets/tbl-row-id-prefixes.md (row_id_prefixes_json_to_md.py).",
  "Rebuild: npm run gen   |   npm run gen:check fails if anything has drifted.",
  "THIS FILE HOLDS NO ROSTER. Which prefixes exist, where each is defined and how many rows it has are read on every run from THREE trees -- docs/spec, docs/development-records and docs/development-rules -- as every `| XX-n |` row of their Markdown and the table caption it sits under. Do NOT write an owning table or a row count here: the join between this file and that walk IS the check, and a copy of either side would be the thing that drifts. A prefix a tree uses and this file does not carry fails the run (unregistered); a prefix this file carries and no tree uses fails it too (dead registration). There is no separate checker.",
  "THE WALK COVERS ALL THREE TREES BECAUSE THE DEFECT IT EXISTS FOR SPANS THEM. One prefix can name a row of the specification and a row of the ledger, and a register that walked only docs/spec could not see the second one.",
  "`mirrored_in` IS A CLAIM THE GENERATOR TESTS, not a way to silence it. A mirror is a tree that records work about rows the owner defines (the ledger's UF-32 is the specification's UF-32), so the generator holds every id in the mirroring tree against the ids the owner defines and fails on the first one the owner never wrote. That test is what separates the real mirrors from the prefixes that mean two different things. `collisions_suppressed` is empty: no prefix stands in two trees with two meanings.",
  "`collisions_suppressed` IS A DEBT, NOT A PERMISSION. Each entry names the prefix, the trees it is used in, the day the suppression was written, why, and what removes it. The printed table shows every one of them, so the count cannot grow in silence, and the generator fails on an entry whose prefix no longer collides -- a suppression cannot outlive its reason.",
  "`words` MAY BE EMPTY, and an empty one is not a gap to be filled by guessing. It says the letters' origin could not be read out of the documents themselves. The defect this registry exists to remove is a spelling that two readers expand two ways, so an invented expansion here would be that same defect with a registry around it. Write `means` and leave `words` empty.",
  "`pending: true` SUPPRESSES THE DEAD-REGISTRATION ERROR, and only that. It is for a prefix registered ahead of the wave that writes its rows; the printed table shows such an entry as a registration with no rows yet, so a reader can see what is suppressed and count it. The generator faults a pending prefix that HAS rows, so the flag cannot outlive its reason. No entry carries it.",
  "One line per prefix, on purpose: a registry is read as a list, and a 128-entry file broken over six lines each cannot be scanned.",
  "The `means` cells are Japanese because they are printed prose of a Japanese specification, held per language the way Chapter 6.2 requires (check 23). `words` is Latin letters and belongs to no language."
 ],
